  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.hmmt-feb-guts-2006-p11.hmmt",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Write \\(\\frac{n^{2}+7 n+136}{n-1}=n+\\frac{8 n+136}{n-1}=n+8+\\frac{144}{n-1}=9+(n-1)+\\frac{144}{(n-1)}\\). We seek to find \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) such that \\(p q=144\\) and \\(p+q+9=k^{2}\\). The possibilities are seen to be \\(1+144+9=154,2+72+9=83,3+48+9=60,4+36+9=49,6+24+9=39\\), \\(8+18+9=35,9+16+9=34\\), and \\(12+12+9=33\\). Of these, \\(\\{p, q\\}=\\{4,36\\}\\) is the only solution to both equations. Hence \\(n-1=4,36\\) and \\(n=5,37\\).",
